excerpt_139_words: "Network models and graph structures represent systems through nodes, edges, connections, pathways, flows, and relationships. Instead of focusing only on isolated variables, they model how entities are linked: people in social systems, species in ecosystems, routers in infrastructure, firms in supply chains, neurons in brains, papers in citation networks, or risks in cascading systems. This article explains how networks translate relational structure into mathematical form through vertices, edges, adjacency matrices, incidence matrices, paths, components, weights, directions, centrality, clustering, communities, and flow. It distinguishes graph structure from network process, examines static and dynamic networks, and shows how topology shapes vulnerability, influence, robustness, contagion, diffusion, and coordination. By treating connections as model objects, network models help analysts reason about structure, dependence, systemic risk, and intervention across science, engineering, policy, technology, economics, ecology, and complex systems practice in accountable decision support today."
